finish('diameter-two-three-coloring-polynomial-time',
 id='TCS-0594',title='Is 3-colorability polynomial-time decidable on diameter-two graphs?',
 area='Graph theory and graph algorithms',criterion='decision',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Does there exist a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm that, for every finite simple connected undirected graph G of diameter at most 2, decides whether there is a map c:V(G)→{1,2,3} such that c(u)≠c(v) for every edge uv?''',
 definitions=r'''The input is the adjacency matrix of an n-vertex graph. Distance counts edges in a shortest path, and diameter is the maximum distance between any two vertices. The graph has no loops or parallel edges. For a singleton the diameter is 0. The algorithm must correctly decide existence of a coloring, including on graphs that are not 3-colorable; it is not supplied with a coloring or a promise of 3-colorability. The color set has fixed size three and there are no vertex lists, precolored vertices, degree bounds, or forbidden-subgraph assumptions. A polynomial-time algorithm has worst-case running time C(n+1)^k for constants C,k independent of G. One may equivalently define a total language that rejects disconnected or diameter-greater-than-two graphs, since the diameter promise can be checked in polynomial time. Restricting to diameter exactly two gives the same polynomial-time question: diameter-zero and diameter-one connected graphs are complete and their colorability is immediate.''',
 answer_criterion='Prove that such an algorithm exists, or that the decision problem is not in P. An NP-completeness proof would establish the conventional hardness classification and imply a negative answer under P≠NP. A subexponential algorithm or an algorithm requiring an additional graph restriction does not settle this question.',
 context=[
  paragraph('The 2019 source discusses several diameter-restricted coloring problems. This card selects its central explicit question, with three colors and diameter two. The same section also asks about triangle-free inputs and about coloring claw-free graphs with an input-dependent number of colors; these are distinct extensions, not additional requirements hidden in this question.','problem'),
  paragraph('Bounding diameter means every pair of vertices is close, but it does not bound the amount of global information encoded by the graph. Diameter-one graphs are complete and trivial to color. Mertzios and Spirakis show that 3-colorability is already NP-complete at diameter three, even for triangle-free graphs. Diameter two is the remaining boundary between these neighboring cases.','baseline'),
  paragraph('A tempting hardness reduction adds a universal vertex to an arbitrary graph. This makes the diameter at most two, but the new vertex consumes a color that none of the old vertices can use. It transfers general 3-colorability to 4-colorability, so it does not prove hardness of the fixed three-color target. Conversely, when a diameter-two instance already has a universal vertex, its 3-colorability reduces to checking that the remaining graph is bipartite.'),
  paragraph('The best general upper bound identified in the July 2026 source is subexponential: Dębski, Piecyk, and Rzążewski give time 2 raised to O(n^(1/3) log² n). Their algorithm combines branching, reductions to 2-SAT, and a structural observation proved probabilistically. The remaining gap is between this bound and polynomial time; the existence of a subexponential algorithm does not exclude NP-completeness.','faster'),
  paragraph('Murakami’s July 2026 preprint proves polynomial-time list 3-coloring when the diameter-two graph contains no four-cycle even as a non-induced subgraph. Its discussion explicitly leaves the general diameter-two case open. The distinction matters: forbidding every four-cycle is stronger than forbidding only induced four-cycles, and neither condition is imposed here.','recent')],
 why='Three-colorability is a basic constraint-satisfaction problem, and diameter is one of the simplest measures of graph connectivity. This single unresolved boundary tests whether very short distances enforce exploitable structure without any sparsity assumption. Either an algorithm or a hardness reduction would explain a fundamental limit of structural restrictions in graph algorithms.',
 importance=dict(score=75,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='A longstanding concrete tractability boundary for a canonical NP-complete problem: two colors and diameter one are easy, while three colors at diameter three are hard. It supports the broader study of algorithms on structurally restricted graphs.'),
 progress=[
  step('2013 conference / 2016 journal','Mertzios and Spirakis obtain a 2^{O(sqrt(n log n))} algorithm for diameter two and NP-completeness for diameter three, including triangle-free inputs.','baseline'),
  step('2019','Paulusma records the diameter-two three-color question as the exceptional unresolved (number of colors, diameter) pair.','problem'),
  step('2021 preprint / 2022 journal',r'Dębski, Piecyk, and Rzążewski improve the diameter-two upper bound to $2^{O(n^{1/3} \log^2 n)}$. Their abstract states that polynomial-time solvability remains open.','faster'),
  step('2026-07','Murakami proves polynomial-time list 3-coloring for graphs of diameter two with no four-cycle as a subgraph. Sections 1 and 7 leave unrestricted diameter-two 3-colorability open.','recent')],
 references=[
  ref('problem','Colouring Graphs of Bounded Diameter, in Graph Colouring: from Structure to Algorithms','Daniël Paulusma',2019,'https://doi.org/10.4230/DagRep.9.6.125','Dagstuhl Seminar 19271; Section 4.3, printed page 139'),
  ref('baseline','Algorithms and Almost Tight Results for 3-Colorability of Small Diameter Graphs','George B. Mertzios; Paul G. Spirakis',2016,'https://doi.org/10.1007/s00453-014-9949-6','Algorithmica 74, 385–414; first online 2014, preliminary SOFSEM 2013 version; diameter-two algorithm and diameter-three hardness'),
  ref('faster','Faster 3-coloring of small-diameter graphs','Michał Dębski; Marta Piecyk; Paweł Rzążewski',2021,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13860v1','28 April 2021; published in SIAM Journal on Discrete Mathematics 36(3), 2205–2224 (2022)'),
  ref('recent','List 3-coloring C₄-free graphs of diameter-2 in polynomial-time','Yukihiro Murakami',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.30282v2','1 July 2026 version; Theorem 1.1 and Sections 1 and 7. C₄-free means no C₄ subgraph, not merely no induced C₄.')],
 status='source_open',status_note='Explicitly open in the July 2026 version of Murakami’s primary paper. Checked through 10 September 2026; no later general resolution located. Recent algorithms with an additional four-cycle exclusion do not settle this card.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
